    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d95">

                <pb facs="0250" n="154" sameAs="frus:frus1872p1/pg154"/>

                <head>No. 95.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Hurlbut to Mr. Fish.</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Bogota</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1872-02-07"><hi rendition="#italic">February</hi> 7, 1872</date>.
                        (Received March 22.)</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 76.]</seg>
                </opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to acknowledge receipt of
                    your dispatch No. 55. The demand for pecuniary damages in the case of the
                    Montijo having been expressly declined by the government of Colombia, and the
                    whole matter remitted by me to the Department of State in my dispatch No. 73,
                    there is no negotiation pending in this legation in reference to the question of
                    damages.</p>
                <p>On the 3d of this month I received a communication from Señor Zapata, informing
                    me that the judge of first instance in Panama charged with the criminal
                    examination against Herrera and Diaz had issued a supersedeas “<hi
                        rendition="#italic">auto de sobresevmiento?</hi> the effect of which, as I
                    understand it, is to stay all proceedings in the matter. From this <pb
                        facs="0251" n="155" xml:id="pg155"/> decision an appeal has been taken to
                    the supreme court now in session, and we are now awaiting its decision. I am not
                    yet informed of the grounds on which the court below stopped the proceedings,
                    and have not taken any special pains to inquire, as the government is fully
                    informed of our views and has repeatedly pledged itself to vigorous
                    prosecution.</p>
                <p>I am, &amp;c,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>S. A. HURLBUT.</signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
